{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019bX9fj7SJNPgkBmAJVs1JW","type":"tool_result","content":"140:\\emph{Question budget.} On the three real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) the naive bias falls monotonically with $N$: $0.051$ at $N=60$ (P5), $0.022$ at $N=200$ (P3), $0.009$ at $N=600$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a). The optimism at $N=60$ is comparable in size to the whole spread between the 24 configurations' pool accuracies, $0.107$ to $0.166$ in the final pool, so at small $N$ the reported advantage of the selected configuration over its alternatives is as large as the differences it is meant to reveal.\n221:\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n239:\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of six primary cases (range $0.009$--$0.031$) and slightly negative only in P1 ($-0.002\\pm0.010$) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a); shrinkage removes most, not all, of the winner's curse. Coverage varies from $0.98$ (P5) and $0.96$ (P1, P2) down to $0.89$ (P4) and $0.83$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}b). Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus an average of over- and under-coverage rather than evidence of calibration in each regime: the fixed width factor over-widens the synthetic and small-$N$ cases and under-widens the largest-$N$ real case. At $N=600$ the target itself is measured on the remaining 900 pool questions, a finite-sample noise that no reporter here models and that matters most where intervals are narrowest; we did not test how much of the P6 shortfall it explains.\n283:\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ and in halving (0.83--0.89), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.\n331:\\textbf{Compute.} The reporter's CPU time per seed (250 calls) averaged $6.2$\\,s, $50$\\,s over the eight sealed seeds. No GPU or API was used in this study. Replaying the baselines takes about three seconds.\n59:      \"claim\": \"Replayed naive Wilson coverage is 0.713 (sd 0.048) and width 0.109; Bonferroni-Wilson coverage 0.962, width 0.190; resampling coverage 0.748, width 0.104 (aggregate over P1-P6). The replay reproduces all recorded baseline interval scores and biases exactly.\",\n162:      \"claim\": \"Reporter CPU time averaged 6.2 s per seed (250 calls), about 50 s over the eight sealed seeds; 2000 reporter calls in total.\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"878436e4-3977-4b60-8d67-4861ccab40b6","uuid":"a52a0d1c-78fd-4f52-95c1-05064360f0f9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:58:08.814Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"140:\\emph{Question budget.} On the three real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) the naive bias falls monotonically with $N$: $0.051$ at $N=60$ (P5), $0.022$ at $N=200$ (P3), $0.009$ at $N=600$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a). The optimism at $N=60$ is comparable in size to the whole spread between the 24 configurations' pool accuracies, $0.107$ to $0.166$ in the final pool, so at small $N$ the reported advantage of the selected configuration over its alternatives is as large as the differences it is meant to reveal.\n221:\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n239:\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of