imism at N=60 (0.051) is of the  ['final.evidence.episodes']\nNaive bias in null worlds: 0.067 in N1 vs 0.049 in P1; 0.034 in N2 vs 0.022 in P3. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nSearcher effect on naive bias: P2 0.054, P4 0.025 vs P1 0.049, P3 0.022; matched paired differences +0.004 (sd 0.012) an ['final.evidence.episodes']\nPer-case EB-IRT vs naive interval scores: P1 0.150 vs 0.264, P2 0.155 vs 0.300, P5 0.192 vs 0.303; reductions of 37-48%; ['final.evidence.episodes']\nP4: EB-IRT 0.156 vs naive 0.161 and Bonferroni 0.165; wins in six of eight seeds; bias 0.031 vs 0.025; coverage 0.89. P6 ['final.evidence.episodes']\nExhaustive-search restricted aggregate: EB-IRT 0.1406, resampling 0.1405, naive 0.2166; halving cases: resampling 0.3916 ['final.evidence.episodes']\nResampling is slightly better than EB-IRT in P3 (0.109 vs 0.116), P5 (0.174 vs 0.192) and N2 (0.102 vs 0.111); EB-IRT be ['final.evidence.episodes']\nResampling over-corrects under halving: bias -0.067 (P2) and -0.031 (P4); coverage 0.44 and 0.61. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nEB-IRT signed bias positive in five of six primary cases (0.009-0.031) and -0.002 (sd 0.010) in P1. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nInterval widths at N=60/200/600: EB-IRT 0.187/0.101/0.065, naive 0.167/0.088/0.050, Bonferroni-Wilson 0.284/0.153/0.088. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nControls: N1 EB-IRT coverage 0.971, interval score 0.128 vs naive 0.354 (naive coverage 0.47); N2 EB-IRT coverage 0.887, ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.constant_control_error']\nWidth factor kappa ranges from 1.43 to 1.63 across the evaluated cases (computed from the reporter's own function). ['final.evidence.episodes']\nDevelopment-setting variants (4 seeds): EB-IRT interval score 0.1480, coverage 0.915, width 0.124; config-by-subject var ['result.top_program_01', 'result.top_program_02', 'result.top_program_03', 'result.total_evaluated_programs']\nThree reporter variants were evaluated once each in the development setting; all three were scored. ['result.total_attempts', 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'result.total_correct_programs']\nEvaluator-recorded wall-clock time averaged 6.2 s per seed (250 episodes, reporter calls plus scoring), about 50 s over  ['final.evidence.episodes']\nInterval score is a proper scoring rule minimized by the 5% and 95% predictive quantiles. ['final.metric.mean_interval_score']\nRelated work: adaptive data analysis, reusable holdout, Ladder, post-selection inference, inference on winners, empirica ['final.qualification.status']\n125:\\textbf{Worlds.} \\emph{Synthetic} worlds draw a base accuracy uniformly from $[0.25,0.75]$ and per-configuration accuracies with spread $0.03$; outcomes follow a Gaussian copula with latent correlation $0.5$ across configurations, and the truth is the selected configuration's accuracy. \\emph{Real} worlds use a cached $1500\\times24$ binary tensor of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct answers on MMLU-Pro (24 prompt configurations: four instruction wordings $\\times$ three demonstration settings $\\times$ two answer-prefix formats; revisions in the Supplement). Each replication samples $N$ questions without replacement, permutes configuration labels, searches, and takes as truth the selected configuration's accuracy on the remaining pool questions. \\emph{Controls}: N1 is a synthetic world with identical configurations; N2 is the real pool with each question's configuration columns reassigned by a random derangement to break configuration effects; Z1 is an all-zero world with $K=8$. In any null world no configuration is better, so a reporter should neither invent signal nor lose coverage.\n140:\\emph{Question budget.} On the three real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) the naive bias falls monotonically with $N$: $0.051$ at $N=60$ (P5), $0.022$ at $N=200$ (P3), $0.009$ at $N=600$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a). The optimism at $N=60$ is of the same order as the spread of the 24 configurations' pool accuracies ($0.107$ to $0.166$ in the final pool); we did not measure how the optimism relates to true between-configuration differences.\n144:\\emph{Searcher and $K$.} Under halving the naive bias is $0.054$ (P2) and $0.025$ (P4), versus $0.049$ (P1) and $0.022$ (P3) under exhaustive search in matched worlds. The matched paired differences ($+0.004\\pm0.012$ and $+0.003\\pm0.008$ across seeds) are small relative to seed spread; aggregated over searcher-specific cases the naive bias is $0.036$ under both. We cannot separate the effect of $K$ because it is confounded with the world (32 synthetic, 24 real, 8 constant).\n221:\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n239:\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of six primary cases (range $0.009$--$0.031$) and slightly negative only in P1 ($-0.002\\pm0.010$) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a); shrinkage removes most, not all, of the winner's curse. Coverage varies from $0.98$ (P5) and $0.96$ (P1, P2) down to $0.89$ (P4) and $0.83$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}b). Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus an average of over- and under-coverage rather than evidence of calibration in each regime: the fixed width factor over-widens the synthetic and small-$N$ cases and under-widens the largest-$N$ real case. At $N=600$ the target itself is measured on the remaining 900 pool questions, a finite-sample noise that no reporter here models and that matters most where intervals are narrowest; we did not test how much of the P6 shortfall it explains.\n283:\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ ($0.83$) and in real-outcome halving ($0.89$), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.\n335:Table~\\ref{tab:suppcase} gives seed-level spread for all nine cases and Table~\\ref{tab:suppbase} the Bonferroni and resampling coverage and width.\n5:      \"source_ids\": [\n12:      \"source_ids\": [\n20:      \"source_ids\": [","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"878436e4-3977-4b60-8d67-4861ccab40b6","uuid":"47819406-113e-4df1-a0e1-d04dff4d0c72","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:58:14.650Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Sealed final evaluation: mean 90%-level interval score of EB-IRT is 0.1449 (primary cases P1-P6, 0.4 synthetic/0.6 real) ['final.metric.mean_interval_score', 'final.metric.combined_score']\nBaseline interval scores in the sealed evaluation: naive Wilson 0.2202, Bonferroni-Wilson 0.1974, resampling 0.2461. ['final.metric.naive_baseline_score', 'final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score', 'final.metric.resample_baseline_score']\nEB-IRT aggregate coverage 0.929, mean width 0.129, RMSE 0.0348, signed bias 0.0121. ['final.metric.coverage', 'final.metric.mean_width', 'final.metric.rmse', 'final.metric.signed_bias']\nNaive aggregate signed bias is 0.0366, i.e. the naive estimate exceeds fresh-question accuracy by about 0.037; EB-IRT re ['final.metric.naive_signed_bias', 'final.metric.signed_bias']\nEvaluator qualification outcome is 'qualified' with status 'completed'; all replications completed (fraction 1.0). ['final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.status', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.metric.completed_replication_fraction']\nNull-control absolute bias 0.0173 and constant-world error 0; null-control coverage averaged over N1 and N2 is 0.929 (N1 ['final.metric.null_control_abs_bias', 'final.metric.constant_control_error', 'final.metric.null_control_coverage', 'final.evidence.episodes']\nSeed-level spread over eight seeds: EB-IRT interval score sd 0.0040, naive 0.0234, Bonferroni 0.0036, resampling 0.0251; ['final.evidence.episodes']\nReplayed naive Wilson coverage is 0.713 (sd 0.048) and width 0.109; Bonferroni-Wilson coverage 0.962, width 0.190; resam ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.naive_baseline_score']\nNaive bias on real exhaustive cases falls with N: 0.051 (N=60), 0.022 (N=200), 0.009 (N=600). ['final.evidence.episodes']\nFinal-pool configuration accuracies range from 0.107 to 0.166 (task manifest); naive optimism at N=60 (0.051) is of the  ['final.evidence.episodes']\nNaive bias in null worlds: 0.067 in N1 vs 0.049 in P1; 0.034 in N2 vs 0.022 in P3. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nSearcher effect on naive bias: P2 0.054, P4 0.025 vs P1 0.049, P3 0.022; matched paired differences +0.004 (sd 0.012) an ['final.evidence.episodes']\nPer-case EB-IRT vs naive interval scores: P1 0.150 vs 0.264, P2 0.155 vs 0.300, P5 0.192 vs 0.303; reductions of 37-48%; ['final.evidence.episodes']\nP4: EB-IRT 0.156 vs naive 0.161 and Bonferroni 0.165; wins in six of eight seeds; bias 0.031 vs 0.025; coverage 0.89. P6 ['final.evidence.episodes']\nExhaustive-search restricted aggregate: EB-IRT 0.1406, resampling 0.1405, naive 0.2166; halving cases: resampling 0.3916 ['final.evidence.episodes']\nResampling is slightly better than EB-IRT in P3 (0.109 vs 0.116), P5 (0.174 vs 0.192) and N2 (0.102 vs 0.111); EB-IRT be ['final.evidence.episodes']\nResampling over-corrects under halving: bias -0.067 (P2) and -0.031 (P4); coverage 0.44 and 0.61. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nEB-IRT signed bias positive in five of six primary cases (0.009-0.031) and -0.002 (sd 0.010) in P1. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nInterval widths at N=60/200/600: EB-IRT 0.187/0.101/0.065, naive 0.167/0.088/0.050, Bonferroni-Wilson 0.284/0.153/0.088. ['final.evidence.episodes']\nControls: N1 EB-IRT coverage 0.971, interval score 0.128 vs naive 0.354 (naive coverage 0.47); N2 EB-IRT coverage 0.887, ['final.evidence.episodes', 'final.metric.constant_control_error']\nWidth factor kappa ranges from 1.43 to 1.63 across the evaluated cases (computed from the reporter's own function). ['final.evidence.episodes']\nDevelopment-setting variants (4 seeds): EB-IRT interval score 0.1480, coverage 0.915, width 0.124; config-by-subject var ['result.top_program_01', 'result.top_program_02', 'result.top_program_03', 'result.total_evaluated_programs']\nThree reporter variants were evaluated once each in the development setting; all three were scored. ['result.total_attempts', 'result.total_evaluated_programs', 'result.total_correct_programs']\nEvaluator-recorded wall-clock time averaged 6.2 s per seed (250 episodes, reporter calls plus scoring), about 50 s over  ['final.evidence.episodes']\nInterval score is a proper scoring rule minimized by the 5% and 95% predictive quantiles. ['final.metric.mean_interval_score']\nRelated work: adaptive data analysis, reusable holdout, Ladder, post-selection inference, inference on winners, empirica ['final.qualification.status']\n125:\\textbf{Worlds.} \\emph{Synthetic} worlds draw a base accuracy uniformly from $[0.25,0.75]$ and per-configuration accuracies with spread $0.03$; outcomes follow a Gaussian copula with latent correlation $0.5$ across configurations, and the truth is the selected configuration's accuracy. \\emph{Real} worlds use a cached $1500\\times24$ binary tensor of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct answers on MMLU-Pro (24 prompt configurations: four instruction wordings $\\times$ three demonstration settings $\\times$ two answer-prefix formats; revisions in the Supplement). Each replication samples $N$ questions without replacement, permutes configuration labels, searches, and takes as truth the selected configuration's accuracy on the remaining pool questions. \\emph{Controls}: N1 is a synthetic world with identical configurations; N2 is the real pool with each question's configuration columns reassigned by a random derangement to break configuration effects; Z1 is an all-zero world with $K=8$. In any null world no configuration is better, so a reporter should neither invent signal nor lose coverage.\n140:\\emph{Question budget.} On the three real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) the naive bias falls monotonically with $N$: $0.051$ at $N=60$ (P5), $0.022$ at $N=200$ (P3), $0.009$ at $N=600$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a). The optimism at $N=60$ is of the same order as the spread of the 24 configurations' pool accuracies ($0.107$ to $0.166$ in the final pool); we did not measure how the optimism relates to true between-configuration differences.\n144:\\emph{Searcher and $K$.} Under halving the naive bias is $0.054$ (P2) and $0.025$ (P4), versus $0.049$ (P1) and $0.022$ (P3) under exhaustive search in matched worlds. The matched paired differences ($+0.004\\pm0.012$ and $+0.003\\pm0.008$ across seeds) are small relative to seed spread; aggregated over searcher-specific cases the naive bias is $0.036$ under both. We cannot separate the effect of $K$ because it is confounded with the world (32 synthetic, 24 real, 8 constant).\n221:\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n239:\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of six primary cases (range $0.009$--$0.031$) and slightly negative only in P1 ($-0.002\\pm0.010$) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a); shrinkage removes most, not all, of the winner's curse. Coverage varies from $0.98$ (P5) and $0.96$ (P1, P2) down to $0.89$ (P4) and $0.83$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}b). Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus an average of over- and under-coverage rather than evidence of calibration in each regime: the fixed width factor over-widens the synthetic and small-$N$ cases and under-widens the largest-$N$ real case. At $N=600$ the target itself is measured on the remaining 900 pool questions, a finite-sample noise that no reporter here models and that matters most where intervals are narrowest; we did not test how much of the P6 shortfall it explains.\n283:\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ ($0.83$) and in real-outcome halving ($0.89$), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.\n335:Table~\\ref{tab:suppcase} gives seed-level spread for all nine cases and Table~\\ref{tab:suppbase} the Bonferroni and resampling coverage and width.\n5:      \"source_ids\": [\n12:      \"source_ids\": [\n20:      \"source_ids\": [","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
